\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We diagnose a dual mechanism underlying step-independent training failure in multi-turn agent RL: policy information loss imposes a constant performance ceiling, while advantage sign misattribution causes cumulative degradation. At $p = 12.5\%$ with $K = 8$, approximately 80.9\% of gradient updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction. A $2{\times}2$ factorial separates the mechanisms: eliminating sign misattribution converts collapse into bounded learning; further removing information loss enables generalization (17.2\% mean OOD at 1.7B, 90.3\% at 8B). Cross-environment validation on ScienceWorld confirms the collapse generalizes.

A systematic evaluation across 11 random seeds reveals that stable training is necessary but not sufficient for OOD generalization: 73\% of seeds avoid collapse, but only 27\% achieve statistically significant OOD improvement over the SFT baseline ($p{<}0.01$, Fisher exact). The remaining non-collapsed seeds are stagnant---they learn in-distribution but do not transfer.

The central insight---that sign errors are more destructive than magnitude imprecision---applies to any agent RL method combining step-level windowing, sparse binary reward, and group-relative advantage normalization. Understanding what distinguishes generalized from stagnant seeds, and developing methods to reliably achieve transfer, are important open questions. A natural next step is $\lambda$-annealing from episode-level ($\lambda{=}1$) to finer-grained credit ($\lambda{<}1$) as the policy stabilizes.

